What is IAM?

IAM (Identity and Access Management) is the AWS service that controls:

Who can log in to AWS
What they can access
What they are allowed to do
Which AWS resources they can use

Think of IAM as the security guard of AWS.

It decides:

"Who are you?"

and

"What are you allowed to do?"

Real World Example

Imagine a company called ABC Technologies.

They have:

20 Developers
10 Testers
5 HR Employees
3 Database Administrators
2 DevOps Engineers
1 CEO

Everyone uses AWS.

Question:

Should everyone have full AWS access?

No.

Why?

Because a tester should not delete production servers.

An HR employee should not access databases.

A developer should not change billing settings.

This is exactly why IAM exists.

AWS Without IAM

Suppose AWS had no IAM.

Everyone knows the AWS account password.

One day a junior developer accidentally deletes:

EC2
RDS
S3

Company loses everything.

Huge disaster.

IAM prevents this.

2. Breakdown of the 4 Key IAM Components
He explains why IAM uses four distinct components rather than just single user logins:

IAM Users: Created for individual team members to log into AWS. They handle authentication.

IAM Policies: Written permissions (in JSON format) attached to users or groups that define what actions are allowed or denied. They handle authorization.

IAM Groups: Collections of users categorized by team or role (e.g., Developers, QA, DB Admins). Attaching policies to a group ensures you don't have to manually update permissions for every single employee individually when permissions change.

IAM Roles: Temporary access credentials used when an external application, server, or another AWS account needs permission to interact with your resources without using permanent human login credentials.

